i strongly believe as an organisation representing young renters in England, we believe the best changes to improve security, affordability and standards while keeping a sustainable rental market are supply-led. Specifically:
- planning reform to speed up permissions, increase density on brownfield sites, and require affordable housing as standard with robust monitoring;
- incentives for build-to-rent and social housing expansion, including long-term leases and rents linked to local affordability;
- use of public land for mixed developments with clear governance to ensure quality and ongoing maintenance;
- stronger standards and enforcement: energy efficiency improvements, damp/condensation fixes, and prompt remedy windows with funded council inspections;
- targeted pilots for rental support: subsidies or vouchers for those most in need, after rigorous evaluation and with sunset clauses;
- balanced security reforms: longer notice periods and clear just-cause protections, plus a public landlord register for accountability.

In my experience, in areas where brownfield redevelopment and build-to-rent projects exist, tenants report greater stability and better condition of homes, while new affordable homes reduce pressure on private rents. These steps would deliver more genuinely affordable homes, improve conditions, and maintain a healthy rental market!
